// LeetCode 313 — five arms against each other and against seven properties
// that follow from the STATEMENT rather than from any implementation.
//
//   A  k-way merge, pointer per prime, ties advance every match
//   B  the same merge, canonicalised so ties cannot arise
//   C  the same merge through a binary heap, ties discarded on the way out
//   D  the closure of {1} under multiplication, via an ordered set
//   E  trial division on every integer in turn — the definition, nothing added
//
// WHAT THE ARMS CANNOT SHARE. A, B and C are three schedulings of ONE
// premise: that every super ugly number x > 1 is p * u for a listed prime p
// and a SMALLER super ugly u, so the sequence can be generated from its own
// prefix. If that premise were wrong all three would be wrong together and
// agree perfectly. D is a genuinely different mechanism — it computes a
// closure rather than merging streams — but it rests on the same premise. E
// rests on nothing: it tests membership one integer at a time and never
// generates anything.
//
// SO WHY ANY PROPERTIES AT ALL, when E is a complete and independent oracle?
// This is worth stating plainly, because it is the thing that decides how much
// the property set is actually worth here.
//
// Wherever E runs, it settles the case outright. Any property that is a
// function of THE ANSWER ALONE is then implied by P1: if arm A returned
// exactly what E returned, and E is right, then A's answer is super ugly, is
// larger than the previous one, and matches every closed form — necessarily,
// with nothing left for a property to catch. Inside E's range the properties
// are LOCALISERS, not detectors. They say how an arm is wrong, not that it is.
//
// E's range is the constraint. It scans every integer up to the answer, so it
// is bounded by the ANSWER's magnitude, while A-D are bounded by n. The two
// diverge fast: with primes = [13] the 12th super ugly number is 13^11, which
// E would need ~1.8e12 iterations to reach and A produces in eleven steps. So
// the case space below is deliberately built to run past E:
//
//   TIER 1 — answer <= 3000. All five arms. E is complete; P2-P7 localise.
//   TIER 2 — answer beyond that, up to 1e15. Four arms and the properties,
//            which are now the only thing standing between a wrong merge and
//            a green run.
//
// The tier-2 count is reported, because a property set whose whole coverage
// sits inside an oracle's range has not been tested for its actual job.
//
// FOUR PROPERTIES NO ARM COMPUTES. Each relates SEPARATE invocations, or
// reaches an answer by a route no arm takes, so each catches a fault that is
// symmetric across every arm — the failure mode all-arms agreement is blind to.
//
//   P3, membership: the returned value is super ugly, checked by factoring it.
//   A-D never factor anything; they multiply. This is the property that
//   catches OVER-generation — a value emitted that does not belong at all —
//   and unlike E it costs one factorisation rather than a scan, so it runs in
//   tier 2 at full strength.
//
//   P5, the single-prime closed form: with one prime, the nth term is exactly
//   p^(n-1). An external answer, computed by repeated multiplication, that no
//   arm's structure can produce by accident.
//
//   P6, permutation invariance: the answer is a property of the SET of primes,
//   so permuting the input array must not change it. Every arm walks the array
//   in one fixed direction, and arm B indexes its canonicalisation by POSITION
//   in that array — so if B's argument had quietly leaned on the primes being
//   sorted by value, this is what would say so.
//
//   P7, superset monotonicity: adding a prime can only make the super ugly
//   numbers denser, so the nth one cannot get larger. It relates two runs over
//   DIFFERENT prime sets, which is the one axis none of the arms varies.
//
// P2 AND P4 ARE THE WEAKER TWO, and it is worth being exact about why rather
// than listing all seven as equals.
//
//   P2, strict increase, is the direct test for the failure mode this problem
//   is actually about — a duplicate emitted because a tie advanced one stream
//   and not the other. That makes it valuable and NOT independent: arm D gets
//   it free from the set, and arm C enforces it with the very comparison the
//   property checks. It is a real detector for A and B and a tautology for C
//   and D.
//
//   P4, no gaps between consecutive answers, is the completeness half of P3 —
//   P3 says nothing invalid is emitted, P4 says nothing valid is skipped, and
//   together they pin the answer exactly. It is also the only property here
//   that costs a SCAN, so it is gated to gaps of at most 1000 and its coverage
//   is reported separately. On a sparse prime set the gap between consecutive
//   terms is enormous and the check simply does not run.

const OR_LIMIT: i64 = 3000;          // tier-1 oracle ceiling
const NMAX: i64 = 45;                // most terms asked of any prime set
const CEIL: i64 = 1000000000000000;  // answers stay below this, so no product overflows
const GAP_CAP: i64 = 1000;           // widest gap P4 will scan

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- oracle ---

fn is_super_ugly(m: i64, primes: ref Vec[i64]) -> bool {
    let mut r = m;
    for i in 0..primes.len() {
        let p = primes[i];
        while r % p == 0 { r = r / p; }
    }
    return r == 1;
}

// Arm E, amortised: one upward scan collects every super ugly number up to
// `limit`, and the nth term for every n in range is then a lookup. Same
// algorithm as `super_ugly_brute.kara`, run once per prime set instead of
// once per (prime set, n).
fn oracle_list(primes: ref Vec[i64], limit: i64) -> Vec[i64] {
    let mut out: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    let mut m = 1;
    while m <= limit {
        if is_super_ugly(m, primes) { out.push(m); }
        m = m + 1;
    }
    return out;
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ arms ---

fn solve_a(n: i64, primes: ref Vec[i64]) -> i64 {
    if n <= 0 { return 0; }
    let k = primes.len();
    if k == 0 { return 1; }
    let mut ugly: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    ugly.push(1);
    let mut idx: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    for i in 0..k { idx.push(0); }
    while ugly.len() < n {
        let mut best = primes[0] * ugly[idx[0]];
        for i in 1..k {
            let c = primes[i] * ugly[idx[i]];
            if c < best { best = c; }
        }
        for i in 0..k {
            if primes[i] * ugly[idx[i]] == best { idx[i] = idx[i] + 1; }
        }
        ugly.push(best);
    }
    return ugly[n - 1];
}

fn solve_b(n: i64, primes: ref Vec[i64]) -> i64 {
    if n <= 0 { return 0; }
    let k = primes.len();
    if k == 0 { return 1; }
    let mut ugly: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    ugly.push(1);
    let mut spf: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    spf.push(k);
    let mut idx: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    for i in 0..k { idx.push(0); }
    while ugly.len() < n {
        for i in 0..k {
            while spf[idx[i]] < i { idx[i] = idx[i] + 1; }
        }
        let mut best = primes[0] * ugly[idx[0]];
        let mut winner = 0;
        for i in 1..k {
            let c = primes[i] * ugly[idx[i]];
            if c < best { best = c; winner = i; }
        }
        ugly.push(best);
        spf.push(winner);
        idx[winner] = idx[winner] + 1;
    }
    return ugly[n - 1];
}

#[derive(Ord, Eq)]
struct Cand {
    val: i64,
    pi: i64,
    ui: i64,
}

fn solve_c(n: i64, primes: ref Vec[i64]) -> i64 {
    if n <= 0 { return 0; }
    let k = primes.len();
    if k == 0 { return 1; }
    let mut ugly: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    ugly.push(1);
    let mut pq: PriorityQueue[Cand] = PriorityQueue.new();
    for i in 0..k { pq.push(Cand { val: primes[i], pi: i, ui: 0 }); }
    while ugly.len() < n {
        match pq.pop() {
            Some(c) => {
                if c.val > ugly[ugly.len() - 1] { ugly.push(c.val); }
                let next = c.ui + 1;
                pq.push(Cand { val: primes[c.pi] * ugly[next], pi: c.pi, ui: next });
            },
            None => { return 0; }
        }
    }
    return ugly[n - 1];
}

fn solve_d(n: i64, primes: ref Vec[i64]) -> i64 {
    if n <= 0 { return 0; }
    let k = primes.len();
    if k == 0 { return 1; }
    let mut frontier: SortedSet[i64] = SortedSet.new();
    frontier.insert(1);
    let mut emitted = 0;
    let mut last = 0;
    while emitted < n {
        match frontier.min() {
            Some(x) => {
                frontier.remove(x);
                last = x;
                emitted = emitted + 1;
                if emitted < n {
                    for i in 0..k { frontier.insert(x * primes[i]); }
                }
            },
            None => { return 0; }
        }
    }
    return last;
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------- case space ---

// How many terms this prime set may be asked for before an answer would pass
// CEIL. Case SELECTION only — it decides which n are tested, never what the
// right answer is.
fn safe_terms(primes: ref Vec[i64]) -> i64 {
    let k = primes.len();
    let mut ugly: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    ugly.push(1);
    let mut idx: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    for i in 0..k { idx.push(0); }
    while ugly.len() < NMAX {
        let mut best = primes[0] * ugly[idx[0]];
        for i in 1..k {
            let c = primes[i] * ugly[idx[i]];
            if c < best { best = c; }
        }
        if best > CEIL { return ugly.len(); }
        for i in 0..k {
            if primes[i] * ugly[idx[i]] == best { idx[i] = idx[i] + 1; }
        }
        ugly.push(best);
    }
    return NMAX;
}

fn subset_of(pool: ref Vec[i64], mask: i64) -> Vec[i64] {
    let mut out: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    for i in 0..pool.len() {
        if (mask / pow2(i)) % 2 == 1 { out.push(pool[i]); }
    }
    return out;
}

fn pow2(e: i64) -> i64 {
    let mut v = 1;
    for i in 0..e { v = v * 2; }
    return v;
}

fn reversed(v: ref Vec[i64]) -> Vec[i64] {
    let mut out: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    let mut i = v.len();
    while i > 0 { i = i - 1; out.push(v[i]); }
    return out;
}

fn rotated(v: ref Vec[i64], by: i64) -> Vec[i64] {
    let n = v.len();
    let mut out: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    for i in 0..n { out.push(v[(i + by) % n]); }
    return out;
}

fn main() {
    let mut pool: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    for p in [2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13] { pool.push(p); }

    let mut cases = 0;
    let mut tier2 = 0;
    let mut p4_checked = 0;
    let mut p5_checked = 0;

    let mut p1 = 0;
    let mut p2 = 0;
    let mut p3 = 0;
    let mut p4 = 0;
    let mut p5 = 0;
    let mut p6 = 0;
    let mut p7 = 0;

    let mut mask = 1;
    while mask < pow2(pool.len()) {
        let primes = subset_of(pool, mask);
        let k = primes.len();
        let oracle = oracle_list(primes, OR_LIMIT);
        let cap = safe_terms(primes);

        // The superset partner for P7: a prime the pool never contains, so
        // `wider` is always a strict superset of `primes`.
        let mut wider: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
        for i in 0..k { wider.push(primes[i]); }
        wider.push(17);

        let mut n = 1;
        while n <= cap {
            let ra = solve_a(n, primes);
            let rb = solve_b(n, primes);
            let rc = solve_c(n, primes);
            let rd = solve_d(n, primes);

            // P1 — the arms agree with each other, and with the oracle
            // wherever the oracle reaches.
            if ra != rb or ra != rc or ra != rd { p1 += 1; }
            if n <= oracle.len() {
                if ra != oracle[n - 1] { p1 += 1; }
            } else {
                tier2 += 1;
            }

            // P2 — strictly increasing, and the first term is 1. The direct
            // test for a duplicate slipping through a tie.
            if n == 1 {
                if ra != 1 { p2 += 1; }
            } else {
                if ra <= solve_a(n - 1, primes) { p2 += 1; }
            }

            // P3 — the answer is super ugly, established by FACTORING it.
            // No arm factors anything. Costs one factorisation, so it runs at
            // full strength in tier 2.
            if not is_super_ugly(ra, primes) { p3 += 1; }

            // P4 — nothing valid was skipped: no super ugly number lies
            // strictly between consecutive answers. The only property here
            // that costs a scan, so it is gated on the gap.
            if n < cap {
                let nxt = solve_a(n + 1, primes);
                if nxt - ra <= GAP_CAP {
                    p4_checked += 1;
                    let mut m = ra + 1;
                    while m < nxt {
                        if is_super_ugly(m, primes) { p4 += 1; }
                        m = m + 1;
                    }
                }
            }

            // P5 — one prime: the nth term is exactly p^(n-1), reached by
            // repeated multiplication rather than by any merge.
            if k == 1 {
                p5_checked += 1;
                let mut want = 1;
                for i in 0..(n - 1) { want = want * primes[0]; }
                if ra != want { p5 += 1; }
            }

            // P6 — the answer depends on the SET, not on the order the primes
            // arrive in. Arm B canonicalises by POSITION, so this is what
            // would notice if that argument had leaned on ascending values.
            if solve_a(n, reversed(primes)) != ra { p6 += 1; }
            if solve_b(n, reversed(primes)) != ra { p6 += 1; }
            if k > 1 {
                let rot = rotated(primes, n % k);
                if solve_b(n, rot) != ra { p6 += 1; }
            }

            // P7 — a denser set cannot push the nth term up.
            if solve_a(n, wider) > ra { p7 += 1; }

            cases += 1;
            n = n + 1;
        }
        mask = mask + 1;
    }

    println(f"cases {cases}");
    println(f"tier2 beyond-oracle cases {tier2}");
    println(f"P4 gap scans run {p4_checked}");
    println(f"P5 closed-form checks run {p5_checked}");
    println(f"P1 arm/oracle disagreements {p1}");
    println(f"P2 monotonicity failures {p2}");
    println(f"P3 membership failures {p3}");
    println(f"P4 skipped-value failures {p4}");
    println(f"P5 closed-form failures {p5}");
    println(f"P6 permutation failures {p6}");
    println(f"P7 superset failures {p7}");

    if p1 == 0 and p2 == 0 and p3 == 0 and p4 == 0 and p5 == 0 and p6 == 0 and p7 == 0 {
        println("DIFFERENTIAL OK");
    } else {
        println("DIFFERENTIAL FAILED");
    }
}
